{"name":"Fallen Rock","hex":"#8b8072","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fallen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of fall"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having dropped by the force of gravity.","Killed, especially in battle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fallen"},{"term":"rock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A formation of minerals, specifically:\n  The naturally occurring aggregate of solid mineral matter that constitutes a significant part of the earth's crust.","The naturally occurring aggregate of solid mineral matter that constitutes a significant part of the earth's crust."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pelt with rocks; to stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rock"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rock Howard","blocks":[{"text":"Rock Howard  is a fictional character from SNK's Fatal Fury series. Previously debuted as a non-playable character in Fatal Fury 3: Road to the Final Victory, Rock makes his first playable appearance in the 1999 fighting game Garou: Mark of the Wolves, a sixth canonical chapter in the series, as the new lead character of the Wolves sub-entry storylines. Rock appears in the series as the son of Geese Howard, the main antagonist from the previous Fatal Fury games, who fell to his death after refusing to be saved by Terry Bogard. However, Terry adopted Rock in order to put his past with Geese behind him, and helped teach Rock how to fight. The mystery behind his missing father leads him to clash with Terry in Mark of the Wolves is further explored in the 2025 sequel Fatal Fury: City of the Wolves. Besides his appearance in Mark of the Wolves, Rock is featured in few games from The King of Fighters series, as well as various crossovers from SNK. The King of Fighters XIV and The King of Fighters XV feature him as downloadable content with the latter incorparting him into the main KOF canon for the first time as the leader of the Garou Team with B. Jenet and Gato, two fellow rivals from his original game."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/RockHowardGarou.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Howard"},{"title":"Fallen (Evanescence album)","blocks":[{"text":"Fallen is the debut studio album by American rock band Evanescence, released on March 4, 2003, by Wind-up Records. Co-founders singer and pianist Amy Lee and guitarist Ben Moody began writing and recording songs as Evanescence in 1994, and after independently releasing two EPs and a demo CD, they signed to Wind-up in January 2001. Several of the songs from their earlier independent releases feature on Fallen. The album was recorded between August and December 2002 in several studios in California. It is Evanescence's only studio album to feature Moody, who left the band in October 2003."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/25/Evanescence_-_Fallen.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallen_(Evanescence_album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/RockHowardsketch.jpg/250px-RockHowardsketch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/71/RockHowardsketch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/RockHowardsketch.jpg/250px-RockHowardsketch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early sketch of Rock by Nobuyuki Kuroki.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARockHowardsketch.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Evanescence_2003.jpg/500px-Evanescence_2003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https